Chapter: Bench EpiShare
  cites Foreword chapter Console

Section: Definitions

 The cell where sharing an epilogue is exactly as expensive as duplicating it.

 Three things have to hold at once and each is deliberate. Two returns, so
 there is a second copy to save. A value live ACROSS a real call, so the
 allocator must give it a callee-saved register and the epilogue is two
 instructions rather than one -- `es-helper` is recursive so the leaf inliner
 declines it and the call survives to force that. And a back-edge laid out
 last, so the layout does NOT end in a return and nothing falls into the
 epilogue.

 At two returns and a two-instruction epilogue, sharing costs two jumps plus
 one epilogue and duplicating costs two epilogues: four instructions either
 way. **Instruction count cannot tell them apart, and never will** -- that
 equality is exact. What separates them is bytes, 12 against 4, and one extra
 executed jump on every return. That is why the harness reports bytes, and why
 this bench's instruction column is expected to sit still while its byte column
 moves.

  es-helper : Integer -> Integer
  es-helper (n) = if n <= 0 then 0 else es-helper (n - 1) + 2

  epishare : Integer -> Integer
  epishare (n) = if n < 0 then es-helper (0 - n) + n else if n == 0 then 0 else epishare (n - 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (show (epishare 5))
    print-line-uni (show (epishare (-7)))
  end

